, 14085, 271, 44364, 14374, 25521, 227, 13023, 21806, 1447, 14085, 198, 59, 79075, 90, 18, 532, 14085, 151645], "output_tokens": 383, "input_tokens": 65, "capped": false, "completion": "We are given that:\n\n$$\n(x - 1) \\cdot f(x) = 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17\n$$\n\nWe are to find the **degree** of the polynomial $ f(x) $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure\n\nLet\u2019s denote the right-hand side as a polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17\n$$\n\nWe are told that:\n\n$$\n(x - 1) \\cdot f(x) = P(x)\n$$\n\nThis means that $ f(x) $ is a polynomial such that when multiplied by $ (x - 1) $, it gives $ P(x) $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use polynomial degree properties\n\nLet\u2019s recall that if two polynomials are multiplied, the **degree** of the product is the **sum** of the degrees of the two polynomials.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\deg((x - 1) \\cdot f(x)) = \\deg(x - 1) + \\deg(f(x))\n$$\n\nWe are told that:\n\n$$\n(x - 1) \\cdot f(x) = P(x)\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\deg(P(x)) = \\deg(x - 1) + \\deg(f(x))\n$$\n\nNow:\n\n- $ \\deg(P(x)) = 4 $ (since the highest power of $ x $ is $ x^4 $)\n- $ \\deg(x - 1) = 1 $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n4 = 1 + \\deg(f(x)) \\Rightarrow \\deg(f(x)) = 3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{3}\n$$", "diagnostic_seconds": 10.019543766975403}
